@* The shaders.
Everything we have computed on the CPU for one texel, the GPU computes for every pixel
of the image, sixty times a second. It does so in two small programs written in HLSL,
the shading language of Direct3D: a \emph{vertex shader}, run once for each vertex of
the mesh, which may move the vertex (displacement mapping) and transforms it to the
screen; and a \emph{pixel shader}, run once for each covered pixel, which builds the
bumped normal and evaluates the lighting.

The program compiles the shaders when it starts and whenever the user presses F5, so
students can edit this file and see the result at once.

<<file:shaders/pbr.hlsl>>=
