@ \label{cbuffer}All the parameters of a frame travel in one constant buffer. HLSL packs
constant buffers in rows of four floats, and a vector may not straddle two rows; the
declarations are arranged accordingly, and the C++ structure that fills the buffer
(<<Constants of a frame, as seen from C++>>) mirrors them field by field. DirectXMath
stores matrices by rows and multiplies row vectors from the left, $\vect p' = \vect p M$;
declaring the matrices |row_major| and writing |mul(p, M)| in HLSL follows the same
convention and spares a transposition.

<<Constants of a frame>>=
cbuffer Frame : register(b0)
{
    row_major float4x4 world;     // object -> world (a rotation)
    row_major float4x4 viewProj;  // world -> clip
    float3 cameraPos;   float bumpStrength;
    float3 lightPos;    float lightIntensity;
    float3 lightColor;  float dispScale;
    float  dispMid;     float dispLod;  float metallic;  float ambient;
    int    viewMode;    int useColor;   int useBump;     int useDisp;
    int    useRough;    float constRoughness;  float2 hoverUV;
    float  hoverOn;     float3 pad;
};

@ The four maps used for rendering occupy the first four texture slots. A single
sampler, with anisotropic filtering and wrap-around addressing, serves them all.

<<Textures and sampler>>=
Texture2D    colorMap : register(t0);  // sRGB view -> linear albedo
Texture2D    bumpMap  : register(t1);  // height used only for shading
Texture2D    dispMap  : register(t2);  // height used to move vertices
Texture2D    roughMap : register(t3);  // specular roughness
SamplerState linearWrap : register(s0);

@ The vertex shader reads the |Vertex| structure of <<Vertex layout>> and passes to the
pixel shader the world position (for the lighting vectors), the normal, the tangent with
its sign, and the texture coordinates. The rasterizer interpolates all of them across
each triangle.

<<Vertex formats of the surface shader>>=
struct VSIn  { float3 pos : POSITION; float3 nrm : NORMAL; float4 tan : TANGENT; float2 uv : TEXCOORD; };
struct VSOut { float4 svpos : SV_Position; float3 wpos : WORLDPOS; float3 nrm : NORMAL; float4 tan : TANGENT; float2 uv : TEXCOORD; };

@ \emph{Displacement mapping} is a single line: the vertex moves along its normal by
$s\,(h-m)$, where $h$ is the height, $s$ the scale and $m$ the mid-level. A vertex shader
cannot use automatic mipmap selection---there are no neighbouring pixels to compare---so
it must choose a level explicitly. The C++ side computes |dispLod| so that the sampled
level has about as many texels as the mesh has vertices, $\log_2(1024/256)=2$ for our
maps; sampling the full-resolution level would make the surface shimmer.

Note what this shader does \emph{not} do: it does not change the normal. A displaced
surface shaded with the normals of the undisplaced one has the right silhouette but the
wrong lighting; that is why displacement maps are almost always paired with a bump or
normal map, and why the program lets you switch them independently.

<<The vertex shader: displacement mapping>>=
VSOut VSMain(VSIn i)
{
    float3 p = i.pos;
    if (useDisp != 0)
    {
        // Displacement mapping: move the vertex along its normal by the height.
        float h = dispMap.SampleLevel(linearWrap, i.uv, dispLod).r;
        p += i.nrm * dispScale * (h - dispMid);
    }
    VSOut o;
    float4 wp = mul(float4(p, 1), world);
    o.svpos = mul(wp, viewProj);
    o.wpos  = wp.xyz;
    o.nrm   = mul(float4(i.nrm, 0), world).xyz;
    o.tan   = float4(mul(float4(i.tan.xyz, 0), world).xyz, i.tan.w);
    o.uv    = i.uv;
    return o;
}

@ This is the formula of \S\ref{bumpnormal}, word for word. The size of a texel is
obtained from the texture itself, and the samples are taken from level~0, as the CPU
does, so that the two computations agree exactly.

<<The normal of a height field, on the GPU>>=
// Normal of the height field z = k*h(x,y), in tangent space, by central differences
// (one texel each side). Same formula as BumpNormalTangent() in src/Inspector.cpp.
float3 BumpNormalTS(float2 uv)
{
    float w, h;
    bumpMap.GetDimensions(w, h);
    float2 t = 1.0 / float2(w, h);
    float dhdx = 0.5 * (bumpMap.SampleLevel(linearWrap, uv + float2(t.x, 0), 0).r -
                        bumpMap.SampleLevel(linearWrap, uv - float2(t.x, 0), 0).r);
    float dhdy = 0.5 * (bumpMap.SampleLevel(linearWrap, uv + float2(0, t.y), 0).r -
                        bumpMap.SampleLevel(linearWrap, uv - float2(0, t.y), 0).r);
    return normalize(float3(-bumpStrength * dhdx, -bumpStrength * dhdy, 1));
}

@ The scene is rendered into an sRGB target: the GPU encodes the linear colours we
compute with (approximately) the power $1/2.2$ when writing them. For the debug views we
want the opposite---the value $0.5$ of a normal component must appear as the byte
$128$---so we pre-apply the inverse power. (The sRGB curve is not exactly a power law,
but the difference is invisible here.)

<<Undo the display encoding of debug colours>>=
float3 SrgbToLinear(float3 c) { return pow(saturate(c), 2.2); }  // so debug colors display unchanged

@ The pixel shader proceeds in the order of the introduction: tangent frame, bumped
normal, material values, and then either a debug view or the lighting.

<<The pixel shader>>=
float4 PSMain(VSOut i) : SV_Target
{
    <<Rebuild the tangent frame in the pixel shader>>

    <<Bump the normal>>

    <<Read the colour and the roughness>>

    <<Choose a debug view>>

    float3 color;
    if (dbg.x >= 0)
    {
        color = SrgbToLinear(dbg);
    }
    else
    {
        <<Evaluate the Cook-Torrance BRDF>>
    }

    <<Mark the probed texel>>
    return float4(color, 1);
}

@ Interpolation across a triangle does not preserve lengths or right angles, so the frame
is rebuilt: the normal is normalized, the tangent is made perpendicular to it again
(Gram--Schmidt), and the bitangent is computed from both and the stored sign, exactly as
<<Public functions of the mesh module>> does on the CPU.

<<Rebuild the tangent frame in the pixel shader>>=
// Tangent frame (TBN). B is rebuilt from N and T with the stored handedness.
float3 N = normalize(i.nrm);
float3 T = normalize(i.tan.xyz - N * dot(N, i.tan.xyz));
float3 B = i.tan.w * cross(N, T);

@ The bumped normal is the tangent-space normal multiplied by the TBN matrix, as in
<<Find the probe on the solid and express the normals in world space>>.

<<Bump the normal>>=
float3 nTS = useBump != 0 ? BumpNormalTS(i.uv) : float3(0, 0, 1);
float3 n = normalize(T * nTS.x + B * nTS.y + N * nTS.z);

@ When the user switches off a map, a neutral value replaces it: a light grey albedo, or
the roughness chosen with a slider. Roughness is clamped away from zero because a
perfectly smooth GGX surface has an infinitely thin highlight, which a point light
cannot show.

<<Read the colour and the roughness>>=
float3 albedo = useColor != 0 ? colorMap.Sample(linearWrap, i.uv).rgb : float3(0.7, 0.7, 0.7);
float rough = useRough != 0 ? roughMap.Sample(linearWrap, i.uv).r : constRoughness;
rough = clamp(rough, 0.04, 1.0);

@ \emph{Debug views} replace the lighting by one of the intermediate quantities, encoded
as a colour. A negative value means ``no debug view''. The UV view is particularly
instructive on the sphere and on the pyramid.

<<Choose a debug view>>=
float3 dbg = float3(-1, -1, -1);
if (viewMode == 1) dbg = pow(saturate(albedo), 1 / 2.2);
else if (viewMode == 2) dbg = n * 0.5 + 0.5;
else if (viewMode == 3) dbg = nTS * 0.5 + 0.5;
else if (viewMode == 4) dbg = rough.xxx;
else if (viewMode == 5) dbg = bumpMap.Sample(linearWrap, i.uv).rrr;
else if (viewMode == 6) dbg = float3(frac(i.uv), 0);

@ \emph{Lighting.} We use the microfacet model of Cook and Torrance in the form that
real-time graphics has made standard. The surface is imagined as a multitude of tiny
mirrors (microfacets); for light to be reflected from the direction $\vect L$ to the
direction $\vect V$, a facet must have its normal along the half vector
$\vect H=(\vect L+\vect V)/\lVert\vect L+\vect V\rVert$. The specular part of the BRDF is
\[ f_s = \frac{D(\vect H)\,G(\vect L,\vect V)\,F(\vect V,\vect H)}
              {4\,(\vect n\cdot\vect L)(\vect n\cdot\vect V)}, \]
where $D$ is the fraction of facets oriented along $\vect H$, $G$ the fraction of them
that are neither shadowed nor masked by others, and $F$ the Fresnel reflectance.
All dot products use the \emph{bumped} normal $\vect n$; that is where the bump map
acts on the image.

<<Evaluate the Cook-Torrance BRDF>>=
// Cook-Torrance microfacet BRDF: GGX distribution, Smith-Schlick geometry, Schlick Fresnel.
<<Directions for the lighting>>

<<The three factors of the specular term>>

<<Add the diffuse term, the light and the ambient term>>

@ The camera and the light are points, so the directions vary across the surface.

<<Directions for the lighting>>=
float3 V = normalize(cameraPos - i.wpos);
float3 L = normalize(lightPos - i.wpos);
float3 H = normalize(V + L);
float NdotL = saturate(dot(n, L));
float NdotV = max(dot(n, V), 1e-4);
float NdotH = saturate(dot(n, H));
float VdotH = saturate(dot(V, H));

@ \emph{Roughness enters here.} The GGX (Trowbridge--Reitz) distribution with parameter
$\alpha=r^2$, where $r$ is the value of the roughness map, is
\[ D = \frac{\alpha^2}{\pi\,\bigl((\vect n\cdot\vect H)^2(\alpha^2-1)+1\bigr)^2}. \]
For small $\alpha$ it is a tall, narrow peak---a sharp highlight---and for $\alpha=1$ it
is constant---no highlight at all, just a sheen. Squaring the map value makes the slider
and the map perceptually more uniform. The geometry term is Schlick's approximation of
Smith's function with $k=(r+1)^2/8$, and the Fresnel term is Schlick's
$F = F_0 + (1-F_0)(1-\vect V\cdot\vect H)^5$, with $F_0=0.04$ for dielectrics and the
albedo itself for metals.

<<The three factors of the specular term>>=
float a  = rough * rough;
float a2 = a * a;
float d  = NdotH * NdotH * (a2 - 1) + 1;
float D  = a2 / (PI * d * d);
float k  = (rough + 1) * (rough + 1) / 8;
float G  = (NdotV / (NdotV * (1 - k) + k)) * (NdotL / (NdotL * (1 - k) + k));
float3 F0 = lerp(float3(0.04, 0.04, 0.04), albedo, metallic);
float3 F  = F0 + (1 - F0) * pow(1 - VdotH, 5);

@ The light that is not reflected specularly, $1-F$, enters the material and comes out
diffusely (Lambert, $\rho/\pi$); metals absorb it. The total is multiplied by the
radiance of the light and by the cosine $\vect n\cdot\vect L$.

One more factor, |horizon|, deserves a word. A bumped normal can face the light even
where the real surface faces away from it, which makes the dark side of the solid
sparkle. Multiplying by a steep ramp of the \emph{geometric} cosine $\vect N\cdot\vect L$
removes the artefact; it is a crude form of self-shadowing.

<<Add the diffuse term, the light and the ambient term>>=
float3 spec = D * G * F / (4 * NdotV * max(NdotL, 1e-4));
float3 kd = (1 - F) * (1 - metallic);
float3 radiance = lightColor * lightIntensity;
// Geometric self-shadowing: no light where the unperturbed surface faces away.
float horizon = saturate(dot(N, L) * 8);
color = (kd * albedo / PI + spec) * radiance * NdotL * horizon + ambient * albedo;

@ Finally, a small magenta ring around the probed texel, drawn in texture space, shows
the student where the texel lies on the solid---and, on the cylinder, that the caps
reuse the coordinates of the side.

<<Mark the probed texel>>=
// Highlight the texel under the mouse in the inspector.
if (hoverOn > 0)
{
    float2 duv = abs(i.uv - hoverUV);
    float r = length(duv);
    if (r > 0.010 && r < 0.016) color = float3(1, 0.1, 0.8);
}

@ \emph{The line shader} draws the arrows of the probe and the marker of the light. Each
vertex carries its own colour, given in display (sRGB) values because that is how one
chooses colours; the pixel shader converts it to linear light for the sRGB target.

<<file:shaders/gizmo.hlsl>>=
// Texture Explorer - colored lines (light marker, normal/tangent arrows).

cbuffer Frame : register(b0)
{
    row_major float4x4 world;
    row_major float4x4 viewProj;
};

struct VSIn  { float3 pos : POSITION; float4 color : COLOR; };
struct VSOut { float4 svpos : SV_Position; float4 color : COLOR; };

VSOut VSLine(VSIn i)
{
    VSOut o;
    o.svpos = mul(float4(i.pos, 1), viewProj);
    o.color = i.color;
    return o;
}

float4 PSLine(VSOut i) : SV_Target
{
    return float4(pow(saturate(i.color.rgb), 2.2), i.color.a);  // colors are given in display (sRGB) space
}
